<!--
    Cast campaign, lever `conststr`: a constant address prints as what it
    addresses.

    Three kinds of address reached PrintC::pushConstant's pointer arm and
    printed as a forced-hex integer behind a cast (or, once globalref names
    program data, as the unnamed global there):

        w_empty: take("") + strcmp(s, "")
                 gcc -O2 stores "" as the terminating NUL of "tab\there\n"
                 (a linker tail merge), and emptystrconst declined the empty
                 literal because the bytes AFTER it are not string data
        w_quote: return "\xa1\ae";
                 gnulib's GB18030 quote: not valid UTF-8, so the string
                 manager refuses it and globalref names it &dat_3000200e
        w_word:  set_slot(&counter, n); counter < 0 ...
        w_name:  set_name(&name, s); strlen(name)
                 the callee's parameter points at `undefinedN`, the caller
                 reads the same storage directly as a long / a char *, and
                 globalref refused the name for the other type

    Two controls keep their form in both passes: w_buf passes a writable
    .data buffer to a char * parameter (never a literal), and w_wide reads
    the storage it passes at another width (keeps the cast).

    The fixture is a non-PIE x86-64 ELF whose file-scope data has no symbol
    (linked with -x). The callees' prototypes are declared with `parse line`
    (set_slot and set_name at a pointer to the unknown word, as a callee-first
    run recovers them) so the constants' types do not depend on the order of
    decompilation. Pass 1 (`option conststr off`) is the bug and pass 2 the
    default.

    Source: decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/conststr_x86_64.c
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
